{{Politics of Cape Verde}}

Presidential elections were held in [[Cape Verde]] on 17 October 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|date=12 January 2021|title=Jorge Carlos Fonseca marca eleições legislativas para 18 de Abril e as presidenciais para 17 de Outubro|url=https://inforpress.cv/jorge-carlos-fonseca-marca-eleicoes-legislativas-para-18-de-abril-e-as-presidenciais-para-17-de-outubro/|url-status=live|access-date=16 August 2021|website=Inforpress|language=pt-PT|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210119015431/https://inforpress.cv/jorge-carlos-fonseca-marca-eleicoes-legislativas-para-18-de-abril-e-as-presidenciais-para-17-de-outubro/ |archive-date=19 January 2021 }}</ref> The result was a victory for [[José Maria Neves]] of the opposition [[African Party for the Independence of Cape Verde]] (PAICV), who received 51.8% of the vote.<ref name="Opposition candidate Neves wins Cape Verde election"/>

==Background==
The outgoing [[List of presidents of Cape Verde|president]] [[Jorge Carlos Fonseca]] was first elected after the [[2011 Cape Verdean presidential election|2011 election]],<ref>{{Cite web|date=22 August 2011|title=Opposition leader wins Cape Verde presidential run-off|url=https://www.france24.com/en/20110822-politics-opposition-leader-fonseca-wins-cape-verde-presidential-runoff|access-date=16 October 2021|website=France 24|language=en}}</ref> and was re-elected in [[2016 Cape Verdean presidential election|2016]] after winning 74% of the popular vote.<ref>{{Cite news|date=3 October 2016|title=President Fonseca wins second term in Cape Verde vote|url=https://guardian.ng/news/president-fonseca-wins-second-term-in-cape-verde-vote/|location=Lagos, Nigeria|access-date=16 October 2021|newspaper=[[The Guardian (Nigeria)|The Guardian]]|agency=AFP|language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web|date=13 October 2021|title=Cape Verde gears towards Presidential election on October 17|url=https://www.africanews.com/2021/10/13/cape-verde-gears-towards-presidential-election-on-october-17/|url-status=live|access-date=16 October 2021|website=Africa News|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211013162018/https://www.africanews.com/2021/10/13/cape-verde-gears-towards-presidential-election-on-october-17/ |archive-date=13 October 2021 }}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The president was elected using the [[two-round system]] by registered voters residing in the country and abroad.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Elections: Cape Verde Presidential August 2011 Round 2|url=https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2201/|access-date=14 February 2021|website=IFES Election Guide|archive-date=3 July 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170703182719/http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2201/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.news24.com/Africa/News/president-fonseca-eyes-second-term-in-cape-verde-vote-20161002|title=President Fonseca eyes second term in Cape Verde vote|access-date=2 October 2016|website=News24|archive-date=7 February 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190207121953/https://www.news24.com/Africa/News/president-fonseca-eyes-second-term-in-cape-verde-vote-20161002|url-status=dead}}</ref> Eligible candidates must be citizens "of Cape Verdean origin, who [[Multiple citizenship|hold no other nationality]]"; over 35 years of age on the date of candidacy; and have resided in the country for three years prior to that date. The application to register as a candidate must be presented to the Constitutional Court for approval, and requires the signatures of at least 1,000 and at most 4,000 electors.<ref name=":0" />

On 27 July 2021 incumbent president [[Jorge Carlos Fonseca]] issued a decree confirming that the election would be held on 17 October, with a second round provisionally scheduled for 31 October.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2">{{Cite web|title=Cape Verde: The presidential election is scheduled to be held Oct. 17; an increase in related campaign rallies is likely from Sept. 30|date=26 August 2021|url=https://www.garda.com/crisis24/news-alerts/517861/cape-verde-the-presidential-election-is-scheduled-to-be-held-oct-17-an-increase-in-related-campaign-rallies-is-likely-from-sept-30|access-date=16 October 2021|website=GardaWorld|language=en}}</ref> Fonseca himself was ineligible to run due to term limits.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|date=16 October 2021|title=Cape Verde votes for president on Sunday|url=https://www.africanews.com/2021/10/16/cape-verde-votes-for-president-on-sunday/|url-status=live|access-date=16 October 2021|website=Africa News|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211016082436/https://www.africanews.com/2021/10/16/cape-verde-votes-for-president-on-sunday/ |archive-date=16 October 2021 }}</ref> The constitution requires candidates to register 60 days prior to the election, thus setting the deadline at 17 August.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|date=27 July 2021|title=Cabo Verde: Presidenciais a 17 de outubro e eventual 2.ª volta no dia 31|url=https://www.noticiasaominuto.com/mundo/1803800/cabo-verde-presidenciais-a-17-de-outubro-e-eventual-2-volta-no-dia-31|access-date=16 August 2021|website=Notícias ao Minuto|language=pt}}</ref>

==Candidates==
The two dominant parties, the PAICV and the [[Movement for Democracy (Cape Verde)|Movement for Democracy]] (MpD),<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Speck|first1=Bruno Wilhelm|last2=Gonçalves|first2=Anilsa Sofia Correia|date=2019|title=O bipartidarismo em Cabo Verde: a dinâmica do surgimento dos terceiros partidos e a magnitude dos distritos|url=http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0103-33522019000200045&tlng=pt|journal=Revista Brasileira de Ciência Política|issue=29|pages=45–88|doi=10.1590/0103-335220192902|issn=2178-4884|doi-access=free}}</ref> both nominated former [[List of prime ministers of Cape Verde|prime ministers]] as their candidates. The PAICV nominated José Maria Neves,<ref>{{Cite web|date=30 May 2021|title=PAICV apoia José Maria Neves como candidato a PR de Cabo Verde|url=https://www.noticiasaominuto.com/mundo/1765398/paicv-apoia-jose-maria-neves-como-candidato-a-pr-de-cabo-verde|access-date=16 August 2021|website=Notícias ao Minuto|language=pt}}</ref> and the MpD nominated [[Carlos Veiga]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=13 May 2021|title=MpD aprova apoio à candidatura de Carlos Veiga a PR de Cabo Verde|url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/mundo/mpd-aprova-apoio-a-candidatura-de-carlos-veiga-a-pr-de-cabo-verde_n1319849|url-status=live|access-date=16 August 2021|website=Rádio e Televisão de Portugal|language=pt|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513193653/https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/mundo/mpd-aprova-apoio-a-candidatura-de-carlos-veiga-a-pr-de-cabo-verde_n1319849 |archive-date=13 May 2021 }}</ref> Veiga ran for the presidency in [[2001 Cape Verdean presidential election|2001]] (when he lost by just 12 votes out of 153,406 cast in the second round) and [[2006 Cape Verdean presidential election|2006]] (losing by a margin of less than 2%).

Besides the two main competitors, five more candidates appeared on the ballot.<ref name=":3" />

==Campaign==
The campaign period began on 30 September and ended on 15 October.<ref name=":2" /> Frontrunners Veiga and Neves both pledged to stabilize the country due to the ongoing COVID-19 crisis,<ref>{{Cite web|last=da Silva|first=Beatriz Ramalho |title=What you need to know about Cape Verde's presidential election|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/10/15/cape-verde-presidential-election-what-you-need-to-know|access-date=16 October 2021|website=Al Jazeera|language=en}}</ref> which heavily destabilized the economy.<ref name=":3" />

==Results==
PAICV candidate José Maria Neves was elected in the first round with over 51% of the vote. This was the first time the PAICV won a presidential election since [[2006 Cape Verdean presidential election|2006]], when [[Pedro Pires]] was re-elected president for a second term.<ref name="José Maria Neves eleito Presidente da República">{{cite web |title=José Maria Neves eleito Presidente da República |url=https://www.rfi.fr/pt/cabo-verde/20211017-projec%C3%A7%C3%B5es-apontam-jos%C3%A9-maria-neves-como-novo-presidente-da-rep%C3%BAblica |website=[[Radio France Internationale]] |access-date=20 October 2021 |language=Portuguese}}</ref><ref name="Opposition candidate Neves wins Cape Verde election">{{cite web |last1=Rodrigues |first1=Julio |title=Opposition candidate Neves wins Cape Verde election |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/opposition-candidate-neves-wins-cape-verde-election-2021-10-18/ |website=[[Reuters]] |date=October 18, 2021|access-date=20 October 2021}}</ref><ref name='José Maria Neves: "É uma grande vitória do povo de Cabo Verde"'>{{cite web |title=José Maria Neves: "É uma grande vitória do povo de Cabo Verde" |last1=Dos Santos|first1=Nélio|date=18 October 2021|url=https://www.dw.com/pt-002/jos%C3%A9-maria-neves-%C3%A9-uma-grande-vit%C3%B3ria-do-povo-de-cabo-verde/a-59534109 |website=[[Deutsche Welle]] |access-date=20 October 2021 |language=Portuguese}}</ref> 

